WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:05.070
Modules in Unreal represent a single, linkable library of functionality.

2
00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:10.070
And there's a number of reasons why we might want to break our game up into modules straight out of the gate.

3
00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:14.070
Whether we want very granular control of what gets deployed with particular targets,

4
00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:18.070
or we've got a game of significant size that really crumbles under the link times,

5
00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:23.070
or maybe we just want to break our game up into reusable pieces. Modules are the way to go.

6
00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:28.359
Simply put, an Unreal game is a single EXE built from module libraries,

7
00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:31.359
the most important of which being the primary gameplay module.

8
00:00:31.359 --> 00:00:38.869
This module acts as the entry point for the EXE, and for the most part it is considered the glue for the rest of the game.

9
00:00:38.869 --> 00:00:42.869
Modules are free to link to other modules depending on what functionality they want.

10
00:00:42.869 --> 00:00:46.869
For editor targets, each module will produce a DLLEach,

11
00:00:46.869 --> 00:00:51.130
which is stored in the project binaries folder of your working directory.

12
00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:55.130
In a shipping target, we produce a single, monolithic executable.

13
00:00:55.130 --> 00:01:00.479
As a quick example of how we might find a game structured when using the module approach,

14
00:01:00.479 --> 00:01:03.479
let's consider spinning up a new project and let's call it FPSGame.

15
00:01:03.479 --> 00:01:08.859
We'd find that Unreal will create a primary gameplay module for us, the FPSGame.dll,

16
00:01:08.859 --> 00:01:10.859
and we've now got our entry point.

17
00:01:10.859 --> 00:01:14.959
Now let's start adding features. Let's say we want guns and grenades.

18
00:01:14.959 --> 00:01:18.959
Well, grenades and guns both kind of rely on the concept of projectiles,

19
00:01:18.959 --> 00:01:22.959
so let's make a new module called projectiles that contains an abstracted framework,

20
00:01:22.959 --> 00:01:24.959
and have both of these modules depend on it.

21
00:01:24.959 --> 00:01:28.959
As with everything in programming, these dependencies can be cyclical,

22
00:01:28.959 --> 00:01:30.959
so we should be cautious when we make these connections.

23
00:01:30.959 --> 00:01:35.959
It's a good rule of thumb to have nothing rely on the primary gameplay module if possible.

24
00:01:35.959 --> 00:01:39.959
Having it act as the glue helps reduce headaches down the line.

25
00:01:39.959 --> 00:01:43.819
So how do we actually create a new module?

26
00:01:43.819 --> 00:01:49.019
A module must at least contain the following, and don't worry, we will expand on each shortly.

27
00:01:49.019 --> 00:01:54.400
It requires a working directory, and this is typically located in the source runtime folder.

28
00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:00.620
Within the project folder structure, a module must be defined within a single folder in alongside your source.

29
00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.620
So as an example to go with the image above,

30
00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:07.620
we'd create a new test module folder within the source runtime directory.

31
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:13.750
We will also require a build.cs file, and this file is necessary to the genetic makeup of the module,

32
00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.750
but don't worry, we will cover this in more detail soon.

33
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:22.750
In our example, we'd create a test module.build.cs file within the working directory that we just spoke about.

34
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.849
And lastly we need a module source file.

35
00:02:25.849 --> 00:02:32.909
This source file defines the module class which inherits from the iModule interface and invokes the implement module macro.

36
00:02:32.909 --> 00:02:40.039
The implement module macro is what signals to the Unreal build tool that there's a new DLL that needs to be generated.

37
00:02:40.039 --> 00:02:44.360
A module is primarily defined by its build.cs file.

38
00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:50.360
It's here that we outline what this module depends on and how this module interacts when it itself is depended upon,

39
00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.360
and there are two very important string arrays that need populating in this file.

40
00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.969
Firstly, we've got the public dependency module names,

41
00:02:58.969 --> 00:03:02.969
and this is a list of what modules this module openly relies on.

42
00:03:02.969 --> 00:03:07.969
In our example from earlier, if guns.dll publicly relied on projectiles.dll,

43
00:03:07.969 --> 00:03:12.969
any import of guns, say we add gun.h to a file in my FPS game,

44
00:03:12.969 --> 00:03:16.969
the linker would also prompt us to import projectiles as well.

45
00:03:16.969 --> 00:03:21.099
Ideally we keep the public dependencies to an absolute minimum.

46
00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:28.099
We want modules to be self-contained pieces of functionality that could theoretically be cut and pasted into a new project with minimal fuss.

47
00:03:28.099 --> 00:03:31.319
Next we've got the private dependency module names,

48
00:03:31.319 --> 00:03:35.319
and these are the modules that this module relies on but internally.

49
00:03:35.319 --> 00:03:42.319
So in our example from earlier again, if guns relied on projectiles but entirely in private or non-API headers,

50
00:03:42.319 --> 00:03:48.319
we can add a private dependency and not force any importing modules to also import projectiles.

51
00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:51.580
But how do we build modules?

52
00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:57.580
Simply adding the code within the module directory is not enough to get the compiler to pick it up and actually compile it.

53
00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:00.580
In order to achieve this, we either need to do one of the following.

54
00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:04.900
Firstly, we can have the primary gameplay module depend on it,

55
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.900
and this can either be as a public or as a private dependency.

56
00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:12.120
Going back to our example with the FPS game, which was the primary gameplay module,

57
00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:17.120
we did not depend on projectiles directly, but we did depend on guns.

58
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.339
These modules are now chained together in a sense and will all get roped into the compile.

59
00:04:22.339 --> 00:04:27.629
If the module doesn't appear anywhere in that chain, it will not get detected by the compiler.

60
00:04:27.629 --> 00:04:31.629
Alternatively, we can add the module to a target.cs file.

61
00:04:31.629 --> 00:04:34.629
And this isn't the typical way of adding a module to the project,

62
00:04:34.629 --> 00:04:39.629
but it is massively powerful if you desire a per-target compilation control.

63
00:04:39.629 --> 00:04:44.629
For example, if you're building a server target, we can add the module to the mygameserver.target.cs file,

64
00:04:44.629 --> 00:04:48.629
which will build it for this target and emit it from the clients.

65
00:04:48.629 --> 00:04:52.629
Similarly, we can add a UI module to the mygameclient target,

66
00:04:52.629 --> 00:04:56.629
and this will emit it from the server targets, which makes sense as a server,

67
00:04:56.629 --> 00:04:59.660
doesn't really have any need for any UI functionality.

68
00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:02.660
This last point is important when considering functionality such as cheats.

69
00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:06.660
If we build a cheat module and emit it from our server and client targets,

70
00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:09.660
then the players can't cheat if the code isn't there to begin with.

71
00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:14.660
We also have the added bonus of stripping out any automation test modules that you may have added

72
00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:16.660
during the development of your project.

73
00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:21.819
Lastly, we also need to add the new module to the list of modules that is held within the UProject file.

74
00:05:21.819 --> 00:05:24.949
This unique file can be opened as a JSON,

75
00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:28.949
and within this file we can add our new module and define two key pieces of data about it,

76
00:05:28.949 --> 00:05:32.949
that being the loading phase and the type of module that we have.

77
00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:34.949
We'll cover what each of these mean in the next slide,

78
00:05:34.949 --> 00:05:38.949
before circling back to the UProject file directly.

79
00:05:38.949 --> 00:05:42.180
Modules themselves can be defined with a load phase and a type,

80
00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:47.180
giving us a massive scope to define how and when a module is allowed to initialise.

81
00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:50.180
Typically they'll fall into one of three categories,

82
00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:53.180
although a great degree of nuance is possible.

83
00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:57.180
You can take a look at the e-host type in the engine for a full list.

84
00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:00.240
Definitions for both e-host type and e-load phase

85
00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:03.240
can be found in the module descriptor.h file.

86
00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.819
The first example of a module type would be the editor type,

87
00:06:07.819 --> 00:06:10.819
meaning it only loads when the editor itself is starting up.

88
00:06:10.819 --> 00:06:13.329
Following that we've got the runtime type,

89
00:06:13.329 --> 00:06:16.329
which means it loads on all targets apart from programs.

90
00:06:16.329 --> 00:06:18.550
From there we have developer tool,

91
00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:21.550
which means it's initialised when compiling for targets with the

92
00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:24.550
BBuildDeveloperTools set to true.

93
00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:28.550
This is very useful for defining modules specifically to hold any automated testing

94
00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:31.550
or any tools that benefit in-house development.

95
00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:34.939
And from there we've got server only and client only types,

96
00:06:34.939 --> 00:06:36.939
and these get spanned up exactly where you'd think.

97
00:06:36.939 --> 00:06:40.939
They get spanned up on dedicated servers and non-dedicated servers, respectively.

98
00:06:40.939 --> 00:06:47.769
Load phases are defined alongside the module type to define when a module spins up.

99
00:06:47.769 --> 00:06:52.800
For instance, editor modules tend to get spanned up during the post engine init phase,

100
00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.800
whereas actual gameplay modules tend to get spanned up during the default phase,

101
00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.800
although the actual loading phase for each module varies depending on the actual use case.

102
00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:03.800
You can see the e-loading phase enum for a full list.

103
00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:09.410
We can see here inside the project file, which was opened just as a JSON file,

104
00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:12.410
we're free to add a number of new modules to the module array,

105
00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:16.410
and it's here that we define the phase and type of the module.

106
00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:20.660
Modules are actually initialised and can quite easily be used as singletons,

107
00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:22.660
with a varying use right across the code base already.

108
00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:27.920
There's nothing stopping us from grabbing a reference to that instantiated module class.

109
00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:32.920
A great example of using modules not just as compartmentalised pieces of functionality within the project,

110
00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:37.779
but as tools in their own right, is when we extend the editor.

111
00:07:37.779 --> 00:07:39.779
Let's consider the following line of code.

112
00:07:39.779 --> 00:07:46.009
The module manager lets us statically grab a reference to the module with the name that we provide.

113
00:07:46.009 --> 00:07:51.069
This name correlates to the name we passed into the implement module macro outlined earlier.

114
00:07:51.069 --> 00:07:56.069
There's nothing stopping us from adding extra functionality to that single mymodule.cpp file,

115
00:07:56.069 --> 00:07:59.069
and using it as a tool or a helper class.

116
00:07:59.069 --> 00:08:04.360
Plugins are a collection of modules broken out into a standalone block of functionality

117
00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.360
that can be turned on or off on a per project basis,

118
00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:10.360
allowing for seamless integration with other projects.

119
00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:14.579
Plugins can be developed to do anything that you would otherwise do directly in the project,

120
00:08:14.579 --> 00:08:19.579
say extending the editor, adding a gameplay system or holding in-house tools.

121
00:08:19.579 --> 00:08:25.579
They also reflect the U project's ability to mix and match different module types depending on your use case.

122
00:08:25.579 --> 00:08:28.970
Let's say we want to add a new asset type to our project.

123
00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:33.970
We can create a plugin that consists of a gameplay module and an editor module.

124
00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:37.000
Our gameplay module would hold the new class definitions,

125
00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:41.000
with the editor module handling the creation of that new asset type in the content browser,

126
00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.000
adding new context menu support,

127
00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:49.350
and invoking any custom editors that we may or may not have wanted to develop alongside our new asset.

128
00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:53.350
We can see what plugins we currently have installed by opening the plugins menu in the editor,

129
00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:57.350
and we can see that the engine comes with a whole suite of plugins already installed,

130
00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:59.350
covering a massive array of use cases.

131
00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:04.350
We can turn off the ones that we don't want, and turn on the ones that we do. It's pretty simple.

132
00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:07.350
It's here that we can invoke the new plugin wizard,

133
00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:12.059
generating the boilerplate for us so we can start fleshing out our own plugin.

134
00:09:12.059 --> 00:09:16.059
The anatomy of a plugin is staggeringly close to that of the U project itself.

135
00:09:16.059 --> 00:09:19.059
While our U project has got all of its code in a source folder,

136
00:09:19.059 --> 00:09:23.179
all of our plugins are stored alongside that source folder.

137
00:09:23.179 --> 00:09:28.179
Inside our plugins folder, we can see any plugins that we've currently got installed on a project level.

138
00:09:28.179 --> 00:09:31.250
In this case, it's our new plugin, My Plugin.

139
00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:36.379
Within My Plugin, we can see how the plugin itself has got the same architecture as the project.

140
00:09:36.379 --> 00:09:40.379
That being a source, intermediate, content, and config folder,

141
00:09:40.379 --> 00:09:43.379
but with a U plugin file instead of a U project file.

142
00:09:43.379 --> 00:09:46.470
The U plugin file, otherwise known as a plugin descriptor,

143
00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:49.470
acts identically to the U project file.

144
00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:53.470
We can open it up as a JSON and add any modules that the plugin relies on,

145
00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:55.470
just like we would with the U project file.

146
00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:58.950
However, we also see that we have a resources folder.

147
00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:03.950
This folder contains any iconography that we may want to appear in the editor.

148
00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:06.950
By default, it contains the default icon that will appear in the plugins menu,

149
00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:08.950
as pictured on the previous slide.

150
00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:14.559
Plugins can also be packaged, allowing for easy project-to-project integration.

151
00:10:14.559 --> 00:10:18.659
If you're authoring a system that isn't specifically tied to your project,

152
00:10:18.659 --> 00:10:20.659
consider making it a plugin instead.
